    "q-109288": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109288",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0)$ and $(-1,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-2xy)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2})\\,dy$ as a repeated integral.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14223852/assets/eac7a442f0a794db0606b95aa722320d099f2bcc4e530fc6b62e9be889335da2.png)\n\n \n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}-2xy$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y-2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-2xy)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x-(2y-2x)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
