    "q-109144": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109144",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2xy-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2x)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(-1,0),$ $(1,0)$ and $(-1,2).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14223852/assets/cb85e51584f0c41fc19210a5ce56289baa652aff3046d5d88c034d187b7e45dd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2xy-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2xy-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2-2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}(2+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2y+{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1-x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2(1-x)+(1-x)^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}-4x+3)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-2{x}^{2}+3x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}-2+3]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-2-3] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
