    "q-109160": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109160",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=x$ and $y={x}^{2}-x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14223852/assets/e788bbcbda4cc715c6989b7de9d08d60e3b0d178eeb9432a342a4c86c4c01b86.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2y-x$ and $Q(x,y)=5x-y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}-x\\le y\\le x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}-x}^{x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}[y]_{{x}^{2}-x}^{x}\\,dx \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =3{[{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(4-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
